counterfactuals.output:
Non-standard neutrino interactions (NSI) modify the flavor-dependent matter potential inside the supernova. For propagation NSI, one can write

\[
H_{\rm mat}=\sqrt2\,G_F N_e
\begin{pmatrix}
1+\epsilon_{ee} & \epsilon_{e\mu} & \epsilon_{e\tau}\\
\epsilon_{e\mu}^* & \epsilon_{\mu\mu} & \epsilon_{\mu\tau}\\
\epsilon_{e\tau}^* & \epsilon_{\mu\tau}^* & \epsilon_{\tau\tau}
\end{pmatrix},
\]

with only differences between diagonal entries physically relevant.

### Effects of non-universal, flavor-diagonal NSI

Differences such as \(\epsilon_{ee}-\epsilon_{\tau\tau}\) change the relative matter potentials and therefore:

- shift the densities and energies at which MSW flavor conversion occurs;
- change whether the conversion is adiabatic;
- create an additional high-density “internal” or \(I\)-resonance if the effective matter-potential difference changes sign;
- alter or introduce a \(\mu\)-\(\tau\) resonance;
- change the sensitivity of the signal to the neutrino mass ordering and to shock-wave passage.

These effects can interchange the original \(\nu_e\), \(\bar\nu_e\), and heavy-flavor spectra. Because supernova \(\nu_x\) spectra generally differ from electron-flavor spectra, the detected flux can become harder or softer and its luminosity can change.

### Effects of flavor-changing NSI

Off-diagonal terms such as \(\epsilon_{e\tau}\) or \(\epsilon_{e\mu}\):

- directly mix flavors in matter, even where standard vacuum mixing would be suppressed;
- control the width and adiabaticity of NSI-induced resonances;
- produce partial rather than complete spectral exchange;
- potentially generate energy-dependent distortions, multiple transition features, or altered spectral splits;
- modify the initial conditions for collective neutrino-neutrino oscillations, sometimes suppressing, moving, or qualitatively changing collective swaps.

Complex off-diagonal NSI may also introduce phase-dependent effects, although extracting those from a single supernova would be difficult.

### Signatures in a megaton water Cherenkov detector

The dominant channel is inverse beta decay,

\[
\bar\nu_e+p\rightarrow n+e^+,
\]

so the clearest observable is a modified \(\bar\nu_e\) positron spectrum. Expected signatures include:

- an enhancement or suppression of the total inverse-beta event rate;
- a harder or softer positron spectrum;
- changes in the mean detected energy and high-energy event fraction;
- abrupt or energy-dependent features associated with shock and reverse-shock propagation through an NSI-shifted resonance;
- altered time evolution during the accretion and cooling phases;
- possible multiple dips or bumps in energy-time distributions;
- a changed neutronization-burst signal, mainly visible through \(\nu e\) elastic scattering, if the original \(\nu_e\) burst is converted into or out of electron flavor.

Subdominant elastic scattering and oxygen interactions provide some sensitivity to \(\nu_e\) and non-electron flavors and can help distinguish a true flavor-conversion effect from a change in the source luminosity.

For a Galactic supernova, a megaton detector would record roughly \(10^5\)–\(10^6\) events, so statistical errors would be small. NSI capable of producing an adiabatic new resonance could therefore cause readily visible, order-tens-of-percent or even order-unity changes. The main limitation would be degeneracy with uncertain supernova emission spectra, standard collective oscillations, the mass ordering, shock dynamics, and detector systematics. Combining water-Cherenkov data with a \(\nu_e\)-sensitive liquid-argon detector and neutral-current-sensitive detectors would greatly improve discrimination.
